#### Comparator verification and governing-law reconciliation

There is no historical Supreme Court merits decision, disposition, opinion or vote in this matter. The historical anchor is the Third Circuit's en banc March 30, 1999 decision, 174 F.3d 305. Its complete primary text, all footnotes, McKee concurrence and Greenberg partial dissent were read from `../sources/chunk5-neutral-early/reform.txt`, mechanically extracted from [the published lower opinion](https://law.resource.org/pub/us/case/reporter/F3/174/174.F3d.305.96-3677.97-3359.html). The neutral packet and decree clarification independently establish the same components and source limit. No appellate merits vote is invented for a sitting Justice.

The lower court affirmed denial of Rule 60(b)(6) relief in No. 97-3359 and Childress's summary judgment in No. 96-3677. It retained paragraph 4 of the December 9, 1996 injunction as written and directed deletion of the separate associational language from paragraph 3. It applied a sufficiently-weighty-interest inquiry, rejecting the four asserted justifications for unequal treatment. McKee favored strict scrutiny. Greenberg accepted Childress's pre-primary equality claim and the Rule 60 denial but would confine the merits and prospective relief to pre-primary cross-filing under Pennsylvania severability law. These are lower-court arguments, not non-Stone commitments or binding Supreme Court law.

The lower court's description of historical Timmons as general anti-fusion approval cannot be imported at that breadth. The entering-law slice establishes the actual April 28, 1997 Timmons holding: Thomas, O'Connor, Scalia, Kennedy and Breyer sustained the specified Minnesota minor/major nomination restriction within its complete system; the distinct two-major-party restriction was not decided. Its Anderson-Burdick analysis preserves review of actual burdens, precise interests and fit. The frozen common pre-primary proposition is compatible with that law: Pennsylvania permits corresponding major-party cross-nominations, and the stated interests do not explain excluding Childress's timely nomination. No universal fusion right or fixed new scrutiny tier follows.

Agostini and Rufo preserve lawful prospective modification under Rule 60(b)(5), with a qualifying change and appropriately limited relief. The present Eshenbaugh motion is under (b)(6), its (b)(4) theory was abandoned, and Timmons did not remove the independent equality premise. Retaining the (b)(6) denial neither adjudicates an unpresented (b)(5) motion nor makes finality an absolute bar to every future adjustment. Childress's separate merits review does not reopen Eshenbaugh's completed merits judgment.

#### Every Associate and component

All eight reach the live prospective dispute; all affirm the (b)(6) denial, support Childress's timely pre-primary equal-protection entitlement and preserve deletion of the independent associational declaration. The following table preserves each distinct remedial conclusion and the personal basis already frozen.

| Justice | Reconciled Childress prospective position | Comparison and basis |
|---|---|---|
| Stevens | Retain the injunction's existing scope for the five section 2870(f) offices | Consistent with the lower majority's remedy; his Timmons dissent and Norman join support willing, sufficiently supported party participation. A hypothetical timing restriction supplies no demonstrated justification for this status-defined bar. |
| O'Connor | Vacate unqualified breadth within Childress review and remand for pre-primary confinement | Corresponds to a genuine lower dissenting alternative, but rests on her own Burdick and Timmons joins preserving lawful selection stages. Agostini preserves the separate finality boundary. |
| Scalia | Same bounded narrowing | His Burdick and Timmons joins support preventive selection rules, and Rufo/Agostini preserve modification through its lawful channel. Equivalent timely filing fails the supplied justification while later applications are not adjudicated categorically. |
| Kennedy | Same bounded narrowing | Norman and his Burdick dissent protect real association; his Timmons and Rufo/Agostini joins distinguish that protection from compulsory accommodation of every later nomination or reopening final claims. |
| Souter | Retain existing scope | His Norman authorship and Timmons dissent require a supported connection between the burden and the claimed governing interest. This record does not justify the status-defined asymmetry after the primary either. |
| Thomas | Same bounded narrowing | His Timmons opinion permits preventive nomination choices without universal fusion approval or a rational-basis shortcut. It supports correcting equivalent timely exclusion while confining the remedy to the proved application. |
| Ginsburg | Retain existing scope | Her full Timmons dissent join rejects the asserted protection of established parties and supports the lower majority's status-based objection. She does not require automatic strict scrutiny to reject these justifications. |
| Breyer | Same bounded narrowing | His Timmons join preserves proportionate regulation of nomination choices; Childress proves unequal simultaneous selection, not an unconditional entitlement to subsequent nominations. |

These are not historical Supreme Court departures because no historical Supreme Court positions exist here. Relative to the lower anchor, five Associates support a narrower remedy and three retain its breadth. The basis for each narrower position is the timing distinction and severability path already supplied to the independent model, coupled with the Justice's stated date-eligible positions; no new fact was introduced in reconciliation. All eight may join a separately stated pre-primary equality holding and Rule 60 holding. Stevens, Souter and Ginsburg do not join the narrowing instruction; the other five do not join an entitlement to every post-primary nomination. No strict-scrutiny proposition receives an inferred join.

#### Operative limits for assembly

The section 2870(f) offices are common-pleas judge, Philadelphia Municipal Court judge, Philadelphia Traffic Court judge, elective school director and justice of the peace. The equality holding does not dictate duplicate votes, party-specific totals, ballot design, or a uniform anti-fusion enactment. It does not reopen a completed election. The independent associational claim is not decided.

The published opinion does not establish whether the December order combined, replaced, duplicated or supplemented the earlier decrees. The narrowing position therefore requires identification and adjustment of relief lawfully within the Childress appeal, expressly preserving Eshenbaugh's final judgment and obligations outside that review. It cannot be described as automatically restoring all post-primary enforcement against the Party. Original decrees and consolidation/amendment entries would resolve the operational relationship; the bounded implementation remand does not need that relationship assumed. The five do not hold that every post-primary restriction is constitutional. The three do not forbid future lawful neutral timing rules.

